# Chapter 1: Introduction.

"""
This folder is a Litex reading of Terry Tao's Analysis I.  It is not a
replacement for the book; it follows the book's order and uses the text as a
pressure test for writing ordinary undergraduate analysis in a small formal
language.

The project is still in early development.

Interactive textbook.

- These `.lit` files are meant to be read as an interactive textbook, not only
  as a static translation.  A reader can run a chapter, see which mathematical
  statements are checked, inspect what each statement introduces or proves, and
  locate the remaining `trust`.
- Because definitions, facts, claims, theorems, proof steps, and chapter-order
  dependencies are all written in structured Litex files, the same source can
  support generated views: a concept-nebula graph of the mathematical ideas, a
  per-statement summary of each line's role, dependency traces showing which
  earlier facts a statement uses, and proof-gap lists for unfinished work.

Starting point.

- Inspired by Professor Terence Tao's Analysis I and his Lean companion
  `github.com/teorth/analysis`.
- Different constraint: no imported analysis package, set-theory package, or
  other prebuilt mathematical library; the development proceeds in book order,
  as if for a reader beginning real analysis.
- Current status: many definitions, examples, and theorem steps are checkable;
  most `trust` marks material that the book leaves as exercises or omits
  as routine, and the rest records work we intend to finish.  This is a test
  release of the roughly 95% version, published so the remaining gaps can guide
  the language feedback loop.

Representative slices.

- Start here: this file explains the project framing and how to read the
  chapter files.
- Early chapters build the book-order foundations: natural numbers, sets,
  rationals, reals, sequences, and limits.
- Chapter 7 is the main series demo: partial sums, Cauchy tails, absolute and
  conditional convergence, rearrangements, alternating series, condensation,
  and root/ratio tests.
- Chapter 11 is the integration demo: interval length, partitions,
  piecewise-constant integrals, upper/lower Riemann integrals, integrability
  criteria, and examples.

What "checked" means.

A successful run means Litex verified the checkable statements in the file,
relative to Litex's builtin rules and any explicit assumptions in that file.
`trust` is not a proof; it is an explicit marker for a proof step that
has not yet been formalized.  Keeping those markers visible is part of the
point of this test release.

This is not a claim that Litex currently has the maturity of Lean and Mathlib.
Lean and Mathlib have a much stronger ecosystem, automation, and checked
library.  The point here is narrower: to see how far a self-contained,
book-order Litex development can go, and where it honestly gets stuck.

Most later chapters pair a short source-facing paraphrase with the
mathematical idea and the corresponding Litex code.  In Litex, `prop` means a
predicate or relation, not a textbook "Proposition"; textbook facts are
usually written as `thm` or local `claim`.

Chapter 1 of the original textbook is mostly motivational.  It reminds the
reader that familiar operations with limits, sums, integrals, derivatives, and
lengths need precise hypotheses, which is exactly the discipline Litex is
meant to make visible.
"""

# Final audit (2026-07-12): no direct non-exercise `trust` or `abstract_prop`
# marker occurs in this introductory chapter.

"""
Formalization audit: silent bridges in Analysis I.

This audit asks a narrower question than "which proofs does the book omit?"
It excludes every step that the source already marks as an exercise, `(why?)`,
`(how?)`, "left to the reader", "easy to show or check", "one can show", a
challenge, or a proof sketch.  Those obligations are already visible to a
human reader.  It also excludes explicitly announced standing conventions and
bridges needed only because this Litex version chooses a builtin carrier in
place of a construction made explicitly in the book.

The entries below record something different: a displayed definition or proof
directly crosses an existence, uniqueness, representation, or domain boundary
without separately deriving, constructing, or typing the bridge.  Some entries
are harmless compression in mathematical prose; others expose a genuine
source-order or stated-domain mismatch.  None casts doubt on the intended
mathematics, but a typed formalization must decide exactly what licenses the
next expression.

1. A "sufficiently small" epsilon still needs a witness.  In the proof that
   the real numbers contain a square root of two, after establishing
   `1 <= x <= 2`, the two contradiction branches directly choose epsilon so
   that `x^2 + 5 epsilon < 2` or `x^2 - 4 epsilon > 2`.  The formal chapter
   supplies the concrete choices `(2-x^2)/10` and `(x^2-2)/8` and checks their
   positivity, that each is strictly less than one, and the required
   inequalities.  This turns an explicit but unexpanded witness choice in
   Chapter 5 into two checked helper lemmas.  The subsequent upper-bound step
   is explicitly marked `(Why?)` in the source and is not counted here.

2. A real upper bound is used as an exponent before real exponentiation is
   defined.  Section 6.7 first recalls that only rational powers `x^q` are
   currently available.  Inside the displayed `x>1` proof of Lemma 6.7.1--not
   the separately omitted `x<1` case--boundedness supplies a real upper bound
   `M` for the rational sequence and the next estimate uses `x^M` and
   `x^(-M)`.  A book-order typed proof must first enlarge `M` to a rational or
   natural upper bound `B`; the already defined quantities `x^B` and `x^(-B)`
   can then play the same role.  The mathematical estimate is unchanged, but
   its domain becomes valid.

3. Finite and countably-infinite summation are silently combined into one
   at-most-countable interface.  Chapter 7 already defines finite-set sums,
   including the empty sum, while Definition 8.2.1 defines a sum over a
   countably infinite set through a bijection `g:N->X`.  Lemma 8.2.3 then uses
   the same notation for an at-most-countable `X`, which may belong to either
   case.  Exercise 8.2.1 asks for the criterion's proof but does not state the
   dispatch between these two existing definitions.  The formal interface
   joins them with an explicit finite-or-countably-infinite branch, so every
   occurrence of the notation has a value on its stated domain.

4. A derivative relation must determine one value before `f'(x0)` is a
   function.  Definition 10.1.1 says that if the difference quotient tends to
   some real `L`, then one writes `f'(x0):=L`.  This directly relies on two
   candidate derivative limits being equal.  The formal chapter first proves
   `derivative_value_unique`, then uses unique existence to introduce the
   derivative-value function while retaining the relation "has derivative
   `L`" as the source definition.

5. Continuity at isolated points does not come from a derivative.  Definition
   10.1.11 requires differentiability only at limit points of `X`, while the
   following corollary, described as immediate, concludes continuity at every
   point of `X` without naming the domain split.  The limit-point case uses
   differentiability; at a non-limit point a sufficiently small neighborhood
   contains no other point of the domain, so continuity holds independently of
   any derivative.  Formalization exposes this second branch as an
   isolated-point continuity lemma before proving the domain-wide result.

6. Interval length must be independent of an endpoint presentation.
   Definition 11.1.8 assigns `b-a` when a nondegenerate bounded interval is
   displayed in one of four endpoint forms.  For this to define a function of
   the interval rather than of the chosen display, any two valid endpoint
   presentations must yield the same endpoints and hence the same difference.
   The formal chapter proves this uniqueness before selecting
   `interval_length(I)`.

7. Containing an open interval does not mean containing its endpoints.
   Definition 11.8.1 assumes `alpha:X->R` and `I` contained in `X`, then defines
   the alpha-length of every nondegenerate endpoint form by
   `alpha(b)-alpha(a)`.  For an open or half-open interval, `I` being a subset
   of `X` does not ensure that both excluded endpoints belong to `X`, so one of
   these applications may be outside the function's domain.  A precise
   interface must require the endpoints (or the closure of `I`) to lie in `X`.
   The current Litex chapter instead uses `alpha:R->R`, which supplies the
   needed endpoint values explicitly.

What this audit shows.

The shortness of this list is intentional.  Exercises, questions, and proof
sketches are important proof debt, but they are not discoveries of
formalization: the source has already told the reader where work remains.  The
distinctive findings are the quiet transitions that ordinary reading rarely
stops to type-check--a witness chosen without a formula, an operation used just
outside its current domain, a selected value whose uniqueness is implicit, or
notation extended to a larger class of objects.  Making those transitions
explicit improves the specification even when the intended mathematics is
clear.

Litex remains inside an explicit trust boundary.  A successful check is
relative to its builtin and inference rules and to any declared axioms; a
`trust` is unfinished work, not evidence.  The claim here is therefore modest:
formalization provides a second reading of the text at the level of domains,
witnesses, selections, and representations that mathematical prose normally
compresses.
"""

"""
Chapter 8 checked addition: independence of enumeration.

After Definition 8.2.1 the source says, without giving the proof, that
Propositions 7.4.3 and 3.6.4 make an absolutely convergent set-indexed sum
independent of the chosen enumeration.  The formal Chapter 8 supplies the
omitted argument: the two enumerations induce a permutation, absolute
rearrangement preserves the sum, and uniqueness identifies the two values.
"""
